# Compiled binary
gohort

# Release output: the binary plus the notices that must ship beside it
build/

# Phantom bridge build artifacts (Mac binary, source lives in apps/phantom/_bridge/)
apps/phantom/_bridge/agent
apps/phantom/_bridge/build/

# Data directory
data/

# Private apps (not tracked in public repo).
# Both entries needed: "private/" matches a real directory; bare
# "private" also matches a symlink named private pointing at a
# private tree elsewhere on disk (dev setup).
private/
private
private.go

# Claude Code local settings
.claude/
fuzz

# Local multi-module development workspace. The replace directives it carries
# are paths on one machine, which is exactly why they no longer live in go.mod —
# committing this would put the same clone-breaking problem back, one file over.
# Start from go.work.example.
go.work
go.work.sum

# Deployment-specific names the scrub gate forbids — a customer, an employer's
# product, an internal tool. NOT committed, and that is the point: a denylist in
# a public repo publishes the very words it exists to keep out. Each developer
# keeps their own; scrub_test.go skips that check when the file is absent and
# still runs the structural ones.
.scrub-denylist
